{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are generally too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends (often ~+1 to +4 EUR). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Overestimates rebounds/mean reversion: after declines, the model predicts a stronger bounce than occurs. Damp mean-reversion strength; cap day-to-day upward corrections following a selloff.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–7) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (days ~8–20) tends to be most overestimated. Apply a larger negative adjustment in the mid-horizon than in the first week.",
    "Trend lag: the model reacts too slowly to sustained declines, staying anchored to prior higher levels. Increase weight on the most recent 5–10 days’ slope; if recent 10-day trend is down, tilt the forecast path downward.",
    "Tail underreaction to sharp upside reversals: when a late, sharp rally occurs (end of horizon), the model can miss the magnitude (under-forecasts the final few days). Add a conditional uplift to the last 3–5 days if realized/spot has turned up strongly in the most recent few sessions."
  ]
}
